# MVVM / WPF conventions

- Use `CommunityToolkit.Mvvm`: `[ObservableProperty]` on a private field for
  bindable state, `[RelayCommand]` on a private method for commands. Don't
  hand-roll `INotifyPropertyChanged` or `ICommand` implementations.
- ViewModels depend only on `PickMeWhatToListen.Application` types
  (`ArtistCatalogService`, `ArtistPickResult`, `Artist`). Never reference
  `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.*` from a ViewModel or View.
- **Always fully-qualify `System.Windows.Application`** in this project
  (e.g. `System.Windows.Application.Current`, and `App : System.Windows.Application`
  in `App.xaml.cs`). The sibling `PickMeWhatToListen.Application` project
  namespace shadows the unqualified `Application` name for every type under
  `PickMeWhatToListen.Wpf`, because C# namespace-member lookup wins over
  using-directives — this is a compile error (`CS0118`), not a style nit.
- The DI composition root lives entirely in `App.xaml.cs`
  (`HostApplicationBuilder` + `services.AddInfrastructure()`). Windows and
  ViewModels are resolved from the container (constructor injection), not
  `new`'d up in code-behind.
- `InvariantGlobalization` is explicitly `false` in this project's `.csproj`
  even though the repo default (`Directory.Build.props`) is `true` — WPF's
  binding engine needs real culture data at startup. Don't remove that
  override.
- Keep code-behind (`*.xaml.cs`) limited to `InitializeComponent`, setting
  `DataContext`, and forwarding lifecycle events (e.g. `Loaded`) into the
  ViewModel. No business logic.
